WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.318
Buffer overflow prevention is often an API design problem.

2
00:00:03.318 --> 00:00:09.216
A function that accepts only a raw pointer gives callers and reviewers very little safety information.

3
00:00:09.216 --> 00:00:15.113
The pointer may refer to one object, an array, output storage, optional data, or legacy ownership.

4
00:00:15.113 --> 00:00:18.431
The API should not force every caller to guess.

5
00:00:18.431 --> 00:00:24.329
Where raw buffers are unavoidable, pair them with explicit length or capacity parameters and clear contracts.

6
00:00:24.329 --> 00:00:27.647
Say whether the length is in bytes or elements.

7
00:00:27.647 --> 00:00:30.964
Say whether the function reads, writes, appends, or formats.

8
00:00:30.964 --> 00:00:35.019
Say whether the function may partially write output when it fails.

9
00:00:35.019 --> 00:00:41.654
Prefer APIs that return owned values, accept containers, or use bounded views when those choices fit the design.

10
00:00:41.654 --> 00:00:47.183
Returning a `std::string`, `std::vector`, or domain-specific buffer object can make ownership clear.

11
00:00:47.183 --> 00:00:50.501
Accepting `std::span` can make a borrowed range clear.

12
00:00:50.501 --> 00:00:52.713
These choices reduce pointer-only ambiguity.

13
00:00:52.713 --> 00:00:55.662
Define what happens when output does not fit.

14
00:00:55.662 --> 00:01:03.771
The policy might be an error return, an exception, truncation with explicit reporting, or a caller-provided retry with the required size.

15
00:01:03.771 --> 00:01:08.563
The important point is that the function does not silently produce ambiguous output.

16
00:01:08.563 --> 00:01:14.461
Ambiguous API contracts become long-term security debt because every caller must preserve a hidden rule.

17
00:01:14.461 --> 00:01:20.359
Safer APIs make the valid call easy, the invalid call harder, and the failure behavior visible.

18
00:01:20.359 --> 00:01:25.520
They are easier to test because the boundary conditions are part of the contract.
